NixOS
=====

The configuration of one machine, the amd desktop, and everything
needed to build it again from an empty disk.  The repository alone
reproduces it: the partition table, the encryption, Secure Boot, the
system and the user half, with nothing done by hand but the answers
the runbook asks for and the secrets listed under By hand.

This file describes what the machine is and why it is that way.  What
each option does in detail is in the comments beside it; the procedure
for installing, and for changing the installed system, is in
install-runbook.md.

system.nix           entry point: the five pins and the imports
configuration.nix    the host: its imports and system.stateVersion
disko.nix            the disk
modules/             the system half, one file per concern
  boot.nix           Secure Boot, loader, kernel, microcode, firmware
  filesystems.nix    fstrim, scrub, I/O scheduler, snapper
  memory-cpu.nix     zram, the reclaim sysctls, the governor
  graphics.nix       mesa and VA-API
  session.nix        Hyprland under uwsm, hyprlock's PAM service,
                     foot, cursor, keymap, fonts
  shell.nix          prompt, ed and EDITOR, no nano, ssh-agent
  firefox.nix        Developer Edition and its policies
  network.nix        hostname, networkd, resolved, nftables, locale
  users.nix          accounts, sudo, the home-manager wiring
  documentation.nix  man pages and the option manual
  packages.nix       what has no module of its own
  compat.nix         nix-ld, for prebuilt dynamically linked binaries
  nix.nix            Nix settings, gc, optimise
home/                the user half, for librefos
  default.nix        imports and home.stateVersion
  hyprland.nix       compositor settings, bar, lock screen, idle
  shell.nix          bash
  git.nix            git, GnuPG, ssh-sendmail
  firefox.nix        the Developer Edition profile
  emacs.nix          Emacs and Gnus
  files/             what no option expresses: gnus.el,
                     ssh-sendmail, and vimrc
scripts/
  reinstall.sh       runbook steps 2 to 10
  apply.sh           a new commit onto the running system
  secureboot-check.sh
install-runbook.md   reinstalling, and changing the installed system
README               this file

hardware-configuration.nix is not in this repository.  It is generated
on the machine, with --no-filesystems, so it carries the initrd module
list and nothing about the mounts or the LUKS device; those come from
disko.nix instead, and nothing is defined twice.


The Machine
===========

Board and Firmware
  MSI A520M-A PRO (MS-7C96), rev 1.0
  AMI UEFI 2.70 (5.17), firmware 1.J0, 2025-04-29
  Secure Boot: enforcing, user mode, this machine's keys only
  Secure Boot Preset: Maximum Security, with an administrator password
  TPM: 2.0, /dev/tpm0 and /dev/tpmrm0 present

CPU
  AMD Ryzen 7 5700G with Radeon Graphics (Cezanne, family 25)
  8 cores, 16 threads, 1 socket
  422 MHz to 4674 MHz, amd_pstate=active on the current cmdline
  L1d 256K, L1i 256K, L2 4M, L3 16M
  Relevant flags: aes, avx2, sha_ni, vaes, rdseed, smep, smap, pku,
  umip, npt, svm (virtualisation available)

Memory
  31 GiB usable
  zram0, 15.6 GiB, the only swap device

Storage
  nvme0n1, 931.5 GiB, Lexar SSD NM610 PRO 1TB (DRAM-less, HMB)
  Controller: Shenzhen Longsys 1d97:1202, driver nvme

Graphics and audio
  Integrated Cezanne Radeon Vega, PCI 1002:1638, driver amdgpu
  Display: single HDMI-1 at 1920x1080
  HDMI/DP audio 1002:1637 and Ryzen HD Audio 1022:15e3, both
  snd_hda_intel
  Codec is Realtek ALC897.

Network
  Realtek RTL8111/8168/8211/8411 rev 15, PCI 10ec:8168, driver r8169
  Requires the rtl_nic firmware blob
  No wireless hardware

Other
  AMD PSP 1022:15df (ccp), AMD IOMMU, ASMedia SATA and USB bridges
  k10temp for thermals

One firmware fault is worth knowing, because it makes Secure Boot look
like it works when it does not.  This board defaults to executing an
image whose signature check fails, which is sbctl's quirk FQ0001 and
affects this model from firmware 7C96v183 onward.  Enrolled keys mean
nothing until Secure Boot Preset is set to Maximum Security in the
setup menu, which it is.  sbctl goes on reporting the quirk, because
it identifies it from the board name and firmware date and cannot read
the preset, so reported state is not evidence here:
scripts/secureboot-check.sh proves enforcement instead, by offering
the boot menu an unsigned binary and watching it be refused.


How It Is Built
===============

Enough of the Nix model to make the rest readable.

Every build output lands in /nix/store under a path whose name begins
with a hash of every input that produced it: sources, dependencies,
compiler flags, build script.  Two builds with different inputs get
different paths and coexist.  Nothing is ever installed over anything
else, which is the whole basis of the rollback and the isolation.

A NixOS system is one derivation.  Building the configuration produces
a system closure; switching to it activates that closure and adds a
boot entry.  The old closure is untouched, which is why the boot menu
carries every generation, and why a wrong change costs a reboot rather
than a repair.

A substituter is a binary cache.  Before building, Nix asks the
configured substituters whether they already have the store path for
this exact derivation; cache.nixos.org is the default.  That is also
the reason this configuration takes stock packages: an overridden
package matches no cached build and is compiled here, along with
everything downstream of it.

No channels and no flakes.  A channel is a pointer to a nixpkgs
revision that has already passed the project build farm, and it moves
on its own; this machine has none.  nix.channel.enable is false and
nix.nixPath points at the pin, so <nixpkgs> in nix-shell and nix repl
resolves to the same revision the system was built from.  Flakes would
give the one thing wanted from them, a recorded revision travelling
with the repository, at the price of an experimental feature;
system.nix gives it with builtins.fetchTarball and a hash.

The entry point is system.nix, the mechanism added in NixOS 26.05,
which both tools understand:

  install:  nixos-install --root /mnt --file /mnt/etc/nixos/system.nix
  rebuild:  nixos-rebuild switch --file /etc/nixos/system.nix

The five pins, all in system.nix, each moved only on purpose:

  nixpkgs       nixos-unstable at 494ce7f, 2026-10-05.  Unstable
                rather than stable, because package freshness is the
                point and stable's six-month cadence does not give it.
                Pinning is what makes unstable comfortable: the
                revision moves when it is moved.
  home-manager  master at fe48612, whose release.json says 26.11, the
                same release as the nixpkgs pin.  Only the CLI is in
                nixpkgs, so the module is a second input.
                home-manager warns when the two releases disagree, so
                this pin moves when nixpkgs crosses a release.
  disko         the v1.13.0 release commit, the same version as the
                disko command nixpkgs packages, so disko.nix is read
                by one disko and not two.  Its module is not in
                nixpkgs either.
  lanzaboote    the v1.1.0 release commit.  It left nixpkgs in 2025,
                and its guide says to track a release.  Since 1.0.0 it
                needs no flakes, which is what made Secure Boot
                affordable here.
  rust-overlay  4748ec2, handed to lanzaboote in place of the one its
                v1.1.0 lock pins, which still writes stdenv.isLinux
                and printed two deprecation warnings on every
                evaluation.  Drop this pin once a lanzaboote release
                carries the fix.

Per-project dependencies do not live on this machine.  A shell.nix in
a project directory declares what that project needs, and nix-shell
there puts exactly those on PATH and nowhere else; the file is
committed with the project, so the environment travels with it and
leaves no trace beyond store paths.  That is why the package lists
below are as short as they are.

Seeing what changed between generations: NixOS keeps built closures,
one per generation, and nothing tracks the configuration files.  A
generation diff shows what changed in the result, not which line was
edited; git is what records the latter.

  nixos-rebuild list-generations
  nix store diff-closures /nix/var/nix/profiles/system-N-link \
                          /nix/var/nix/profiles/system-M-link


The System
==========

Boot and Secure Boot            modules/boot.nix

  lanzaboote replaces the systemd-boot installer with one that signs.
  It installs the same systemd-boot, signed, and a signed image per
  generation in EFI/Linux in place of the unsigned Type #1 entries, so
  the menu and rollback through it carry over unchanged.  The keys
  live in /var/lib/sbctl, and the encrypted disk is the only
  protection they get.

  No key is copied off the machine and none is kept.  The routine for
  any install is to delete the firmware's Secure Boot variables,
  install, and let the machine make and enrol new keys with no step by
  hand.  autoGenerateKeys runs sbctl create-keys on a boot where
  /var/lib/sbctl/keys does not exist; autoEnrollKeys exports them once
  into /boot/loader/keys/auto and sets secure-boot-enroll force, which
  systemd-boot acts on while the firmware is in Setup Mode and ignores
  afterwards.  Microsoft's certificates are not enrolled: there are no
  expansion cards, so no option ROM needs verifying, and the runbook
  reads the TPM event log from the installer, before anything is
  erased, to confirm that is still true.

  The boot menu's editor is off.  NixOS leaves it on for backwards
  compatibility and recommends false, because it gives root through
  init=/bin/sh.  With Secure Boot enforcing lanzaboote's stub already
  ignores an edited command line; this closes the menu side as well.

  configurationLimit is 10, which caps how many generations keep a
  kernel and a signed image on the 1 GiB ESP.  Garbage collection
  bounds the store separately, in nix.nix, and the two are not the
  same limit.

  amd_pstate=active on the command line, amdgpu in the initrd, AMD
  microcode updated, and hardware.enableRedistributableFirmware, which
  pulls linux-firmware whole.  nixpkgs ships no per-vendor subset, and
  extracting one by hand is not worth a local derivation on a 1 TB
  disk with a deduplicated, compressed store.

  No Unified Kernel Image is built separately: on the Arch system a
  UKI existed to give Secure Boot one signable artifact, and here the
  signed image per generation is that artifact.

The disk                        disko.nix, modules/filesystems.nix

  One description, two jobs.  The disko module turns disko.nix into
  fileSystems and boot.initrd.luks.devices for the running system, and
  the disko command reads the same file on a reinstall to erase,
  partition, encrypt, format and mount.  Partitions are found by GPT
  partition name under /dev/disk/by-partlabel/, not by UUID, which is
  what lets one file describe a disk that does not exist yet.

    nvme0n1p1  ESP        1 GiB     vfat, label BOOT, on /boot,
                                    fmask and dmask 0077
    nvme0n1p2  cryptroot  the rest  LUKS2, then btrfs labelled nixos

  Subvolumes, all with noatime, compress=zstd:3, ssd, discard=async
  and space_cache=v2:

    @           /
    @home       /home
    @nix        /nix
    @log        /var/log
    @snapshots  /home/.snapshots

  @nix is separate so that rolling @ back never rolls the store back
  with it, which would leave the generation being rolled back to
  without the paths it needs.  @log stays so a rollback does not
  destroy the record of what went wrong before it.  @snapshots is
  top-level rather than nested under @home, because services.snapper
  never runs snapper create-config and so nothing else would create
  it.

  The LUKS container has no key file: a reinstall asks for the
  passphrase at the terminal, and it is the one typed at every boot.
  allowDiscards passes TRIM through the container, so fstrim and
  discard=async reach the drive instead of stopping at the filesystem,
  which on a DRAM-less budget NVMe is the case worth caring about.
  What it costs is that somebody holding the powered-off disk learns
  which blocks are in use; accepted on a machine that does not leave
  the room.

  On top of that: fstrim, a monthly scrub of /, the I/O scheduler set
  to none for NVMe and bfq for anything rotational, and snapper
  timeline snapshots of /home only.  Generations already cover
  everything the configuration owns, atomically and with a boot entry,
  and they never touch /home.  System rollback is a NixOS feature,
  data rollback is a Btrfs feature, and neither is asked to do the
  other's work.

Memory and CPU                  modules/memory-cpu.nix

  zram at half of RAM, zstd, priority 100, with the four reclaim
  sysctls that belong with it: swappiness 180, watermark_boost_factor
  0, watermark_scale_factor 125 and page-cluster 0.  Those numbers are
  correct only because the swap device is RAM, and carrying them
  without zram would be a mistake.  On 31 GiB this is insurance
  against one runaway process rather than tuning; it costs nothing
  idle, because the device allocates only what it holds.

  The CPU governor is performance.

  zswap is left alone.  nixpkgs compiles it in and leaves it off at
  runtime, so the zswap.enabled=0 parameter an Arch system needs would
  be a no-op here.

Graphics and audio              modules/graphics.nix

  hardware.graphics.enable and LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=radeonsi, and that is
  all of it.  mesa carries both radeonsi and RADV, so there is nothing
  to add to extraPackages.  Measured in the session: OpenGL 4.6 on
  radeonsi, Vulkan 1.4 on RADV, and VA-API decoding H264, HEVC, VP9,
  MPEG2, VC1 and JPEG, with H264 and HEVC encode.  There is no AV1 in
  either direction; that is the silicon, one generation short of it,
  and no configuration can add it.

  There is no audio configuration at all, deliberately.  wireplumber
  selects the analog sink unconfigured, so the UCM workaround, the
  card index and the unmute loop the Arch system carried all have
  nothing to do.  Naming no card index is also what makes probe order
  stop mattering.  pipewire itself arrives through
  services.graphical-desktop, which programs.hyprland pulls in.

The session                     modules/session.nix, home/hyprland.nix

  Hyprland under uwsm, with no display manager.  The session starts
  itself when librefos logs in on tty1, from the login shell:

    if uwsm check may-start -q; then
      exec uwsm start -e -D Hyprland hyprland.desktop
    fi

  That is upstream's own launch line.  -D names the desktop and -e
  makes it exclusive, so XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP is Hyprland alone, which
  is what the portal reads to choose a backend; hyprland.desktop runs
  start-hyprland, so a compositor crash is recovered and a crash while
  locked comes back locked.  The nixpkgs uwsm module can express
  neither, which is why there is no programs.uwsm.waylandCompositors
  block; withUWSM sets programs.uwsm.enable, and everything the
  session needs lives there.

  may-start is the guard and is not optional: uwsm's environment
  preloader sources the profile itself, so an unguarded exec can loop.
  It passes only in a local login shell, not as root, on VT 1, with
  graphical.target active and no graphical session already running.
  Every other VT is a plain console, which is the way back in if the
  session ever fails to start.

  The system owns the parts of the session that are not the user's:
  hyprlock's PAM service, foot through programs.foot, the cursor, the
  keymap, the fonts, and the D-Bus registration gcr's prompter needs
  in order to draw the GPG passphrase window.  The compositor's own
  settings, the bar, the lock screen's layout and the idle rules
  belong to the user and are in home/hyprland.nix.

    foot        monospace:size=12 on black with #bbbbbb text.  The
                sixteen ANSI colours are deliberately untouched: red
                for an error is a value, not decoration.
    cursor      DMZ-Black at 24, through Xcursor.  24 is a floor,
                since DMZ is drawn at 24, 32 and 48 and Hyprland draws
                the nearest image without scaling it.
    keymap      us/intl, set both in services.xserver.xkb, which is
                what gives the console its keymap, and in the
                compositor's own input block, or dead keys stop
                working and accented characters go with them.
    console     Terminus at 8x16, ter-v16n, which is the kernel's own
                cell size, so only the letterforms change.
    fonts       terminus_font_ttf, whose family is "Terminus (TTF)",
                first in defaultFonts.monospace with DejaVu behind it,
                so one decision covers every client that asks for
                monospace.  DejaVu, Liberation, Noto and the colour
                emoji are there for the text coverage a browser and a
                document viewer need.
    tools       wmenu, grim, slurp, wl-clipboard, pavucontrol.  There
                is no wallpaper daemon: there is no wallpaper, and
                Hyprland's own is turned off rather than covered up.

Shell and editors               modules/shell.nix, home/shell.nix

  One prompt for every account, replacing the NixOS default, which
  begins with a newline and so leaves a blank line after every
  command's output.  The colour goes with it: bash's \$ already prints
  # for root and $ for everyone else, which is the same information
  stated rather than signalled.  The escape that sets the window title
  stays, because the bar reads it.

  Two editors: Emacs, for librefos, and GNU ed for everything else.
  EDITOR is ed, set as a session variable, so the systemd user manager
  and the services under it see it and not only login shells.  nano is
  switched off, since NixOS installs it and sets EDITOR=nano
  otherwise.  vim is not installed; the Arch vimrc is kept in
  home/files/vimrc, verbatim and imported by nothing.

  ssh-agent runs as a user service, with SSH_AUTH_SOCK exported from
  /etc/profile before the session starts, so Hyprland and everything
  under it find it.  Keys are still added by hand with ssh-add.

Network                         modules/network.nix

  The host is amd.  systemd-networkd with one .network unit for
  enp34s0 on DHCP, systemd-resolved, and the firewall on its nftables
  backend, already denying incoming.  NetworkManager would buy nothing
  on a machine with no wireless hardware.  The trap to remember is
  setting systemd.network.enable while useNetworkd is false, which
  lets networkd and dhcpcd fight over the same interface.

  America/Sao_Paulo and en_US.UTF-8.

Users and privilege             modules/users.nix

  One account, librefos, in wheel, plus root.  The organisation that
  might have been separate accounts is directories under one home
  instead: the only thing separate accounts buy over that is isolating
  one context's compromise from another, which is not wanted here,
  and keeping toolchains apart is already answered by per-project
  shell.nix.

  users.mutableUsers is false, so the user and group files are
  replaced wholesale on activation and the only way to add a user or
  change a shell is to edit this repository as root.  The consequence
  to know: passwd stops working, and both hashes come from
  /etc/passwords/root and /etc/passwords/librefos, which are placed by
  hand and never committed.  Those files have to exist before the
  first activation or nobody can log in, root included.

  sudo stays, with execWheelOnly, so the binary is not executable at
  all outside wheel.  nix.settings.trusted-users is left at its
  default of root: a user not on that list cannot add substituters,
  cannot import unsigned store paths, and cannot influence what the
  daemon builds for anybody else.

  What a normal user can still do is install into their own profile,
  which changes nothing system-wide and needs no root.

  home-manager is wired in here, as a NixOS module, with useGlobalPkgs
  so it builds from this system's pkgs rather than a second nixpkgs,
  and useUserPackages so home.packages become the user's system
  packages instead of a nix-env profile.  There is no
  backupFileExtension: an existing file where a link should go stops
  activation with "would be clobbered", and that is wanted, because a
  stray file should be looked at rather than renamed out of sight.

Documentation                   modules/documentation.nix

  A machine that answers its own questions offline.  man.cache.enable
  and dev.enable both default to false and are both on here, which is
  what makes apropos and man -k work and what puts the library pages
  in section 3.  nixos.includeAllModules makes man 5 configuration.nix
  cover every module this machine actually runs, not only the base
  ones.  man-pages and man-pages-posix are the Linux and POSIX API
  collections.

  So: man for the system and the APIs, info for the GNU tools, man 5
  configuration.nix for every option set here, nixos-help for the
  manual, nixos-option to query a live value, and apropos to find any
  of them without knowing the name first.

Nix itself                      modules/nix.nix

  nix-command is on, for the newer CLI; flakes are not.  Garbage
  collection runs weekly and deletes generations older than 30 days,
  and store optimisation runs weekly beside it.  The tension is worth
  restating: generations are the rollback mechanism and garbage
  collection deletes them, so that window is the safety net being
  sized.  Revisit it with real data on how fast the store grows here.

Packages                        modules/packages.nix

  Stock, unpatched, and only what the workflow names.  Anything with a
  NixOS or home-manager module is enabled through that module instead,
  because that is also where it is configured.  What is left with no
  module of its own:

    tmux                on its own defaults, no configuration at all
    sbctl               makes, verifies and enrols the Secure Boot keys
    wireguard-tools     wg and wg-quick for the tunnel to the vps,
                        which is configured by hand in /etc/wireguard
                        so its keys never enter this repository
    curl wget file tree ripgrep htop

Compatibility                   modules/compat.nix

  nix-ld.  NixOS has no Filesystem Hierarchy Standard, so a prebuilt
  binary compiled for a generic glibc Linux and hardcoding
  /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2 as its interpreter fails to start here.
  nix-ld installs a stub at that path, resolving against the libraries
  listed in libraries instead.  Hit first through pnpm, which fetches a
  native binary of itself the same way; the fix is general and will
  cover the next tool that does this too.

The User Half
=============

home/, built by home-manager as part of the system and never on its
own: modules/users.nix imports it as home-manager.users.librefos, so
nixos-rebuild builds it with the rest and a bad generation rolls back
from the boot menu like any other.

  bash        history controls, the ls and grep aliases, set -o vi.
              The prompt, EDITOR and the SSH agent are the system's,
              above, and are not restated here.
  git         name, email, send-email through ssh-sendmail, and
              commits and tags signed by default.
  GnuPG       the agent with pinentry-gnome3, so the passphrase is
              asked for in a window and every caller gets the same
              prompt: git in a terminal, Emacs, and git started from
              Emacs.  A curses pinentry has to be told which terminal
              to draw in, and a program started from the session has
              none.  The window is drawn by gcr's prompter, which
              modules/session.nix registers with D-Bus.
  Emacs       emacs-pgtk, the pure GTK build, native on Wayland, which
              already is everything the Arch emacs-git PKGBUILD asked
              for.  Gnus reads ~/.gnus.el and nothing else, so that
              stays a file.
  Firefox     the dev-edition-default profile: 172 preferences and
              no stylesheet.  Developer Edition itself and the
              enterprise policies are the system's, in
              modules/firefox.nix, because programs.firefox there
              writes /etc/firefox/policies/policies.json.  The tab
              strip is Firefox's own, at the top and unstyled; the
              theme is the built-in compact dark one.  uBlock Origin is
              installed by policy on the first run, with its button in
              the nav bar.
  Hyprland    the compositor's settings in Lua, the waybar bar, the
              hyprlock lock screen and the hypridle rules.  Idle locks
              the session after five minutes and turns the monitor off
              half a minute later; a manual lock runs loginctl
              lock-session, so it takes the same path as the timeout
              and as sleep.  The bar and the wmenu launcher are one
              strip: Terminus at 12 pixels in both, written as 12px on
              each side so neither goes through a point size, and the
              bar's height is the 16 pixels wmenu computes for itself,
              so the launcher covers the bar rather than sitting above
              it with its text a few pixels off.

Two files stay files, because no option expresses their content:
gnus.el and ssh-sendmail, which is a script and is
installed through writeScriptBin so it is on PATH without adding a
directory to it.  vimrc is kept and imported by nothing.


Conventions
===========

Options over files.  Where NixOS or home-manager has an option for
something, the option is used; a file is kept only where no option can
express the content.  A NixOS module is preferred to a home-manager
one when the program reads /etc, because then there is one statement
for the machine rather than one per user.

Stock packages.  No overlay and no patches.  Everything is what the
binary cache already has, which is also what keeps a rebuild to a
download.  An overlay remains the mechanism for the first package that
genuinely needs one, and nothing here makes adding it harder.

Values over visuals.  Nothing on screen has to be decoded before it
can be read.  The bar is words and numbers, not icons; the palette is
black, grey and white; there are no gaps, rounding, blur, shadows,
animations, wallpaper or window borders.  The exception is deliberate:
the terminal's ANSI colours are left alone, because a program printing
red is saying something.

Defaults are learned rather than overridden.  The keybindings are
Hyprland's own, not habits carried in from another window manager.

Nothing secret is committed.  The password hashes, the GPG secret key,
~/.authinfo.gpg, the SSH keys and the WireGuard tunnel are all placed
by hand and listed under By hand.

Comments cite what they were read from, and option names are checked
against the revisions system.nix pins rather than against memory.


Installing and Changing
=======================

install-runbook.md is the procedure; the scripts only save the typing,
and each stops with STOP: at the first thing that is not as expected.

  scripts/reinstall.sh          runbook steps 2 to 10, in the NixOS
                                minimal installer, from the copy of
                                this repository unpacked in step 1.
                                It checks sbctl's option-ROM condition
                                before anything is erased, runs disko,
                                installs, and leaves only disko's
                                question, the LUKS passphrase and the
                                two passwords to the user.  It never
                                reboots.
  scripts/secureboot-check.sh   after a reinstall, in two runs around
                                one reboot: it reads the enforcing
                                state and then proves it, by writing a
                                boot entry that points at the unsigned
                                kernel and watching the firmware
                                refuse it.  The entry is removed
                                straight after.
  scripts/apply.sh              a new commit onto the running system.
                                Every .nix file parses, the new
                                configuration evaluates in a staging
                                copy beside this machine's own
                                hardware-configuration.nix, and
                                nixos-rebuild dry-build says what
                                would be downloaded and what would be
                                compiled.  Nothing changes until that
                                has been read and answered yes; then
                                the previous /etc/nixos is saved to
                                /root/etc-nixos.before-apply and the
                                switch runs.

The repository reaches the machine one of two ways, neither of them a
public paste: as a git archive of a commit on the Ventoy stick the
installer boots from, or, once the machine is running, cloned from the
git server on the vps over the WireGuard tunnel.  Both carry only
committed files.

Anything touching the mounts or the initrd is worth evaluating before
it is built, which costs two commands and no reboot:

  nix-instantiate --eval --strict --json /etc/nixos/system.nix \
    -A config.fileSystems
  nix-instantiate --eval --strict --json /etc/nixos/system.nix \
    -A config.boot.initrd.luks.devices

By hand, after any install:

  /etc/passwords/root and /etc/passwords/librefos, written from
  mkpasswd before the first activation, the directory 700 and the
  files 600.  The runbook types each password a second time against
  the salt in its file, because mkpasswd asks once and never confirms,
  and with mutableUsers false a silent typo locks both accounts at
  once.  It is recoverable from the installer with nixos-enter.
  The GPG secret key, and ~/.authinfo.gpg.
  The SSH keys and ~/.ssh/config, with its vps host.  ssh-add once per
  session.
  The WireGuard tunnel in /etc/wireguard.

Secure Boot needs nothing by hand: the firmware's Secure Boot
variables are deleted before installing, and the machine makes and
enrols its own keys over the first two boots.


Deliberately Absent
===================

Each of these was decided rather than forgotten.

  TPM auto-unlock.  The mechanism transfers and the machine has the
  chip, but sealing to PCR 7 is only honest while Secure Boot
  enforces, and a passphrase at boot is strictly stronger than the
  alternative it would replace.  Deferred, not rejected; revisit if
  the machine ever leaves the room, alongside the disposal case that
  justifies LUKS here.

  Impermanence.  Adopted and then removed, after two of its four steps
  had run.  NixOS has no built-in way to wipe the root, so on Btrfs
  the wipe is hand-written code in the boot path, rewritten here for
  systemd stage 1, which is the kind of unofficial workaround this
  configuration does not carry.  The declarative alternative, / on
  tmpfs, was rejected on its own terms.  What it would have solved,
  undeclared state accumulating where nobody is looking, is real but
  is not what this migration was for; the subvolume layout stays
  compatible with it, so adopting it later is a configuration change
  and not a reinstall.

  An overlay.  It turned out empty.  emacs-pgtk already is what the
  Arch emacs-git PKGBUILD asked for, and llama-cpp's override exists
  as the packaged llama-cpp-vulkan, which dispatches on the CPU at
  runtime instead of being built with -march=native.

  nixos-hardware.  Per-model profiles, and there is no A520M-A PRO
  profile; the generic AMD pieces it would supply are the one or two
  options already written here.

  An X11 session.  Wayland, with no second session kept to retreat
  into.  XWayland is switched off at runtime, in the compositor
  settings, rather than through programs.hyprland.xwayland.enable,
  which would override the package with a variant nobody else builds
  and so compile Hyprland here on every pin move.

  UFW, reflector, mkinitcpio, genfstab, and a package-cache subvolume.
  Each has either no equivalent or no need: the firewall is a module,
  substituters replace mirrors, stage 1 is systemd by default, the
  mounts come from disko.nix, and there is no pacman cache to keep out
  of snapshots.

  A natively tuned build.  -march=native would defeat substitution:
  every store path would miss the binary cache and the machine would
  compile the world, which is the same trade on any distribution.


Open
====

None of it blocking.

  The rust-overlay pin, to be dropped once a lanzaboote release
  carries the stdenv fix it exists for.

  Moving the pins.  It is an ordinary apply: edit the URLs and hashes
  in system.nix, dry-build, switch.  Hyprland's configuration syntax
  moves across minor releases, so a pin move is likeliest to need work
  there; the configuration is already Lua, which is what 0.57 wants.

  AV1, absent in this hardware's video engine.  Whether to care is a
  question about what this machine plays, not one Nix can answer.

  The laptop, the intel profile, out of scope until now.


